australia has reopened its international  border for the first time since the  start of the coronavirus pandemic  several states are now allowing fully  vaccinated citizens to freely enter the  country without quarantining for a in a  hotel for two weeks the first arrivals  touched down on monday there were  emotional scenes at sydney airport as  families reunited  [Music]  [Applause]  oh my gosh  they've been waiting a long time to hug  their loved ones again  after months of video calls and missed  milestones these australians have  finally returned home  a little bit scary and exciting i've  come home to see my mom because she's  not well  so um  it's all anxious and excitement and i  love her heaps and i can't wait to see  her oh how long have you been away two  years oh man yeah it's been a long time  this is a big deal  we've been locked out and to finally  have this chance to come back it's just  amazing so  we're a bit um overwhelmed and we just  can't wait to see our family it's going  to be amazing  australia sealed its borders in march  2020 citizens wishing to return had to  overcome an array of hurdles from  gaining special entry permission to  forking out thousands of dollars in  hotel quarantine fees  but the states of victoria new south  wales and canberra have now vaccinated  more than 80 percent of their adult  populations deemed high enough to allow  quarantine free entry to citizens who  have also had the jab  for some it's too little too late  i'm fully vaccinated i've been  vaccinated for  um  months now like in australian citizen  and i haven't been able to go back into  my own country and i've been able to  travel to every other country except  australia and i haven't seen my dad in  two years and now he's very very sick so  it's like appalling how australia has  treated us  this is the first stage in a plan that  will eventually include international  workers and tourists as the country  adapts to living with the virus rather  than trying to eliminate it  but for now these returning citizens are  the lucky ones vaccination rates and  reopening plans vary from state to state  meaning some australians will still have  to wait a little longer to hold their  loved ones again  [Music]  dw reporter rochelle harrison plesso is  at sydney airport and sent us this  report  it's the end of so-called fortress  australia after more than one and a half  years or 583 days to be exact the  country has lifted its controversial  travel ban from today fully vaccinated  australians will no longer have to apply  for an exemption to travel overseas and  fully vaccinated australians can fly  into sydney and melbourne the country's  two largest cities without having to go  through hotel quarantine so as you can  imagine that has set the scene for some  emotional reunions and lots of relief  here today at sydney airport  it's good feels real good it's glad that  um the borders are re-opening and  life in a new normal can move forward  how does it feel to have your big  brother home good  i love it we have to get back to real  life  can't stay on the island all our lives  i'm heading over to washington dc  to see my daughter who i haven't seen  for two years so i'm pretty excited  about that  prime minister scott morrison recently  said it was time to give australians  their lives back and that's what it  feels like as the country slowly opens  up again some 45 000 australians are  stranded abroad and have registered as  wanting to come home since the pandemic  began well they are the top priority  these eased restrictions first applying  to them the government then hopes to  welcome back fully vaccinated skilled  workers and international students  before the end of the year finally  they'll be followed by international  tourists so the reopening is gradual and  cautious rochelle harrison pless dw  sydney  you
 

